From a94183694f345c6d24671570d72bf8a15eaab77a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:15:16 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] settings_ui: the jail's limits are steppers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo proposal §6.62, ask 85 (2026-11-22). 0141's limit rows on the Agent
Sandbox page were free-text inputs. They are now Zed's own NumberField
steppers (settings_ui::components::number_field): − / +, Shift steps
further, Alt finer, and a click on the value still takes typing.

Memory and disk growth are one size type each, stored in MiB and shown in
their largest whole unit (4G, 1536M, 1T), accepting K, M, G and T and a
bare number as MiB. What a stepper shows is what it writes, always with a
suffix: ai-jail reads a bare number as bytes and refuses a size under
1 MiB or with a B suffix. Steps: memory 1 GiB (Shift 4 GiB, Alt 256 MiB),
disk 5 GiB (20 GiB, 1 GiB), CPU 100 % -- one CPU (400 %, 10 %), capped at
this machine's CPUs -- and processes 256 (1024, 16). Every change still
goes through LimitField::parse, the limit's own rule, before it is queued.

The reset button stays: "unset, use the default" differs from 0, which
turns a limit off. A click would rewrite the settings file each time, so
the page writes once, 400 ms after the last click, and shows the pending
value meanwhile, so the next click counts from it; a reset cancels a
waiting click. "No limit" shows beside the title while none is in force.
The types and the debounce live in a new file, agent_jail_steppers.rs,
beside agent_jail_limits.rs (agent_jail_page.rs is past 1800 lines).

Tests (pages::agent_jail_page::agent_jail_limits::steppers::
agent_jail_steppers_tests, new): display in the largest unit, K/M/G/T
parsing and what ai-jail refuses, every value a stepper can show passes
the limit's rule and round-trips, the steps, the start from the setting
(bytes rounded up to MiB), and clicks in a row writing once, after the
last one, with the last value. Red seen against stubs with the old
free-text behaviour (write at once, no units); the round-trip property
holds on the stub by construction.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
